.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt KLDLOAD-TPM-SEAL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kldload-tpm-seal
.Nd let the TPM unlock the encrypted ZFS root, but only while Secure Boot is as it was
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Cm seal
.Op Fl -passphrase-file Ar file
.Op Fl -pool Ar pool
.Nm
.Cm status | remove
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Secure Boot on its own is not enforced: turn it off in the firmware and
the machine boots anyway.
.Nm
seals the passphrase of the ZFS root's encryption root to the machine's
TPM, bound to PCR 7, which measures the Secure Boot state.
While that state is unchanged the machine unlocks itself at boot, with no
passphrase; turn Secure Boot off, change its keys, or update firmware in a
way that changes them, and the TPM refuses, and the boot asks for the
passphrase as before, with a warning.
.Pp
The passphrase itself is sealed, not a key derived from it, so the pool
keeps its passphrase: the TPM stands in front of it, never instead of it.
.Pp
At boot, the dracut module
.Pa 91kldload-tpm-unlock
runs before ZFS's own passphrase prompt: if the root's key is not loaded
yet, it has the TPM unseal the credential straight into
.Ql zfs load-key .
If that fails it says why on the console and the prompt appears as usual;
it never stops a boot.
.Pp
It must run as root.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm seal
The default.
In order: finds the encryption root of the pool's boot environment; takes
the passphrase from
.Fl -passphrase-file ,
or asks for it twice on the terminal; checks that it opens the pool with a
dry
.Ql zfs load-key -n ,
so a wrong one is refused now rather than at the next boot; seals it with
.Xr systemd-creds 1 ,
TPM2 only, PCR 7, to
.Pa /etc/kldload/tpm/zfs.cred ,
and unseals it once to prove it can; rebuilds every initramfs, so it
carries the credential, and checks that the newest one does.
It refuses a root that is not encrypted, or not by passphrase, a machine
without a usable TPM2, and a system without the dracut module.
With Secure Boot off it seals anyway, and warns that turning it on later
will ask once.
.It Cm status
Whether the root is encrypted, the Secure Boot state, whether a
credential is sealed, whether the TPM would release it now, and whether
the initramfs carries it.
.It Cm remove
Delete the credential and rebuild the initramfs without it; every boot
asks for the passphrase again.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -passphrase-file Ar file
Read the passphrase from
.Ar file
instead of the terminal.
Nothing in kldload passes one yet; see
.Sx CAVEATS .
.It Fl -pool Ar pool
The pool whose boot environment's key to seal; default the pool the
running root is on.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the description and usage and exit 0.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /etc/kldload/tpm/zfs.cred
The sealed passphrase, mode 0600, on the encrypted root itself, and
copied into the initramfs.
.It Pa /usr/lib/dracut/modules.d/91kldload-tpm-unlock/
The boot side.
It is included in an initramfs only when the credential exists.
.It Pa /boot/efi/EFI/BOOT/initrd.img
The initramfs that boots, on the EFI partition, rebuilt with the others.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
Done.
.It 1
It failed, or was refused; the message says why.
.It 2
A usage error.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Printed on a kldload desktop on 2026-09-30, whose root is encrypted and
not sealed:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-tpm-seal status
encryption root: rpool
secure boot:     SecureBoot enabled
sealed:          no
# (exited 0)
$ kldload-tpm-seal status
kldload-tpm-seal: run as root (it reads the pool and writes the initramfs)
# (exited 1)
.Ed
.Pp
Sealing, not run for this page; it asks for the passphrase twice:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-tpm-seal
# then: kldload-tpm-seal status  -> sealed: yes, tpm releases it: yes,
# in initramfs: yes
.Ed
.Pp
After a firmware update changes the Secure Boot keys, the next boot asks
for the passphrase once; then seal again:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kldload-tpm-seal
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr systemd-creds 1 ,
.Xr dracut 8 ,
.Xr kldload-secure-boot 8 ,
.Xr zfs-load-key 8
.Sh HISTORY
Written after it was shown on 2026-09-18 that a machine with Secure Boot
turned off in the firmware booted anyway.
.Pp
On 2026-09-30, writing this page: with more than one pool imported, it
took the first pool in alphabetical order as the boot pool, whose key it
would then have sealed; it takes the running root's pool since.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
It is installed on Fedora and Enterprise Linux machines and does nothing
until someone runs it: first boot does not seal automatically.
.Pp
It had not been run on real hardware when this was written; the
.Cm status
above is the only output shown here that a machine printed.
.Pp
Only Fedora and the Enterprise Linux family, whose initramfs is built by
.Xr dracut 8 ;
Debian's initramfs-tools has no hook for it yet.
.Pp
PCR 7 does not change when the kernel, the initramfs or ZFSBootMenu
change, only when Secure Boot's own state does; with Secure Boot on,
those are held to their signatures instead.
